n\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\fi-1\ri391\sl244\slmult1\sb236\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Each time you make a change to a Suspension Data input, the program checks how that change affects static calculations, like spring\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 length,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 roll\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 center\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 position,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 etc.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 It\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 also\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 moves\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 suspension\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 through\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 some\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 standard\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 movement,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 like\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 1\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 inch\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 of\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 dive,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 to\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 see the effect on 3 critical specs:\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Camber, Caster and Toe In.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 change is called "gain" and is labeled in the suspension layout as Camber Gain, Caster Gain and Toe In Gain (often called Bump Steer).\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 The program's default standard movement is 1 inch of dive.\expnd12\expndtw62  \expnd0\expndtw0 However, you are able to change this to some combination of dive and/or roll.\expnd12\expndtw62  \expnd0\expndtw0 These Gain numbers make for handy,\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 quick checks on the impact of a certain modification.\expnd12\expndtw61  \expnd0\expndtw0 For example, if you move a ball joint, and Toe In Gain increases, you know immediately that this change will increase Bump Steer.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri506\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 If\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 you\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 change\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 standard\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 movement\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 from\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 1"\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Dive\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 to\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 1/2"\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 dive,\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 then\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Gain\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 numbers\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 reported\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 will\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 drop\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 by\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 approximately half.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 You can only make comparisons on Gain numbers calculated for equal standard motions.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb3\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri506\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 During this standard movement, the program also checks on how much the springs (and shocks and roll bar, if any) compress (twist in the case of the roll bar) for this standard movement.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Knowing how far the spring compresses for a given amount of wheel movement is\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the precise way\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the program\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 calculates\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 spring (and shock and roll bar, if any) Motion Ratio.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Unlike the Gain numbers, the Motion Ratios calculated are corrected for changes in the standard motion.\e