\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb37\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb10\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li359\ri811\sl244\slmult1\sb1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Note that the current suspension settings are compared to some \f8\b1\i1 saved \f0\b0\i0 suspension file.\expnd12\expndtw62  \expnd0\expndtw0 If you want to make a comparison graph of a suspension with several different modifications, you must save each modification as a separate file.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 For example, say you want to compare the original suspension file called WCSample to WCSample with\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 the steering rack raised 1 inch.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 If the current file is a copy\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 of the WCSample with the 1\'94 modification, then\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 you would graph the current suspension (copy\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 of WCSample with the rack raised 1\'94) and add the original WCSample.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Simple enough.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li359\ri917\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 But, if you want to graph WCSample with the rack raised 1\'94 with WCSample with the rack dropped 1\'94, you have to save either of these suspensions with this modification at the main screen under some new name, like WCSample Raised 1.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Then you can modify\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the rack position to a 1\'94 drop from\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 the original position.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Make a graph of the current suspension (with a 1\'94 drop).\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 You\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery725\footery744\margtsxn1100\margbsxn940\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\sl244\slmult1\sb111\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 now have the option of graphing this suspension with either the\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 original rack position (WCSample)\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 or with the rack raised 1\'94 (WCSample Raised 1).\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb8\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri365\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Figure 4.23 is Graph 4.22 with the added Suspension File for comparison, \f8\b1\i1 and \f0\b0\i0 with the Secondary Type of suspension motion shown in Figure 4.19 set to Roll.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Each secondary suspension motion, in this case Roll at -2, -1, 0, 1 and 2 degrees, produces a separate graph line.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 You can see it can get quite confusing when\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 you get so many\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 different types of suspension motion graphed, especially for 2 different suspensions.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Usually you don\'92t want too many levels of Secondary motion, because it adds too many additional graph lines.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par
