\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb6\f2\fs19\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f2\fs28\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb19\f2\fs28\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s10\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li360\sl240\slmult1\f2\fs28\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 FLLD\expnd-2\expndtw-8  \expnd0\expndtw0 (Front\expnd-1\expndtw-7  \expnd0\expndtw0 Lateral\expnd-1\expndtw-7  \expnd0\expndtw0 Load\expnd-2\expndtw-8  \expnd0\expndtw-2 Distribution)\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li360\ri861\sl244\slmult1\sb123\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 When a car makes a turn, weight is transferred from the inside tires to the outside tires.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 However, how this weight transfer is split between the front and rear has a huge impact on the feel and handling of the car.\expnd12\expndtw60  \expnd0\expndtw0 If more weight transfer occurs on the\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 front of the car, the outside front tire is\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 being \'93overworked\'94 more than the rear\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 outside tire, causing less cornering traction\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 at the front.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This is more likely to produce understeer or a push.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Very simplistically, if the Front Lateral Load Distribution is 50%, that means the weight transfer split between front and rear is the same, and that should produce neutral handling.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 The book \'93Race Car Vehicle Dynamics\'94 by Milliken and Milliken (with assistance by Terry Satchell) discusses this concept in detail.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li360\sl-226\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 This\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 concept\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 is\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 similar\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 to\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 idea\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 of\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 balancing\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 front\expnd-1\expndtw-6  \expnd0\expndtw0 and\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 rear\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 roll\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 angles\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 presented\expnd-1\expndtw-5  \expnd0\expndtw0 in\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 recent\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 magazine\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw-2 articles.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li360\ri834\sl244\slmult1\sb124\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Figure A 6.2 shows the program displaying the FLLD for the current vehicle (currently 44%, which would tend to have Moderate Oversteer) during the transition between releasing the brakes and going to the throttle, at the apex of the turn (transistion between braking and power).\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 During braking and under acceleration, the weight transfer is much more complicated, and the FLLD concept can not be as easily applied.\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery725\footery744\margtsxn1100\margbsxn940\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb187\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li846\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20
