\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri506\sl244\slmult1\sb106\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Figure A6.3 is displayed if you click on\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 the \'93Find\'94 button shown in Figure A6.2.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This \'93Find FLLD\'94 screen lets you find a certain Front Lateral Load Distribution (FLLD), which can be a good indication of how the car will handle at the apex or transistion of the turn (no power, no braking).\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb2\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri6691\sl244\slmult1\sb1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Pick the 'Adjust' factor to tell the program what vehicle component(s) you want to adjust.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Enter your desired 'For This FLLD', then click on the 'Find Now' button.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 For perfect theoretical 'Balance', the FLLD should be 50%.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 However, from experience, Milliken suggests a target 'starting\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 point'\expnd-1\expndtw-6  \expnd0\expndtw0 FLLD\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 value\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 of\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 5\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 percentage\expnd-1\expndtw-4  \expnd0\expndtw0 points higher than the percent weight on the front tires (100% - Rear Wt % = Front Wt %).\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 By default, the program will load in this value, but you can change it to anything else you want.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Higher FLLDs tend to make the car tighter, with more understeer.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Lower FLLDs tend to make the car looser, with more oversteer.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri6735\sl244\slmult1\sb225\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 There are several ways to obtain a certain Front Lateral Load Distrbution percentage.\expnd8\ex